export const notesPart4 = {
  "85": "# 85. Data Structures & Algorithms (DSA) Introduction\n\n## 1. What is DSA & Why Does it Matter?\nA **Data Structure** is a specialized format for organizing, processing, retrieving, and storing data in computer memory efficiently. An **Algorithm** is a finite, step-by-step sequence of unambiguous instructions to solve a computational problem.\n\n```\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS HIERARCHY                              |\n+-------------------+--------------------+--------------------+---------------------+\n| Constant O(1)     | Logarithmic O(logN)| Linear O(N)        | Quadratic O(N^2)    |\n| Array index lookup| Binary Search      | Linear Search      | Bubble/Nested loops |\n| Stack push / pop  | Balanced BST lookup| Single array pass  | Matrix pairwise sum |\n+-------------------+--------------------+--------------------+---------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Big-O Asymptotic Notation\n* **O(1) - Constant Time**: Execution time is completely invariant to input size.\n* **O(log N) - Logarithmic Time**: Each iteration cuts problem space in half.\n* **O(N) - Linear Time**: Time scales directly proportional to input length N.\n* **O(N log N) - Linearithmic Time**: Standard for optimal comparison sorts (MergeSort, QuickSort).\n* **O(N^2) - Quadratic Time**: Nested loops over N items.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates Time & Space Complexity benchmarking in Java\npublic class DsaComplexityDemo {\n    // O(1) Constant Time Operation\n    public static int getFirstElement(int[] arr) {\n        return arr[0];\n    }\n\n    // O(N) Linear Time Operation\n    public static int computeSum(int[] arr) {\n        int sum = 0;\n        for (int num : arr) sum += num;\n        return sum;\n    }\n\n    // O(log N) Logarithmic Time Operation (Binary Search on sorted data)\n    public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {\n        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;\n        while (low <= high) {\n            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n            if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;\n            else if (arr[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;\n            else high = mid - 1;\n        }\n        return -1;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] sortedData = {10, 25, 40, 55, 70, 85, 99};\n\n        System.out.println(\"O(1) First Element : \" + getFirstElement(sortedData));\n        System.out.println(\"O(N) Total Sum     : \" + computeSum(sortedData));\n        System.out.println(\"O(log N) Search 55 : Index \" + binarySearch(sortedData, 55));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nAlways evaluate time and space complexity trade-offs: picking the optimal data structure (e.g. HashMap O(1) vs Array O(N) search) dramatically elevates enterprise performance.",
  "86": "# 86. 1D Array in Java (Declaration, Allocation & Memory Layout)\n\n## 1. 1D Array Fundamentals\nIn Java, an array is an **object** allocated dynamically in Heap memory. It holds a contiguous, fixed-size sequence of elements sharing a single homogeneous data type.\n\n```\nStack (Reference)              Heap Memory (Contiguous Storage)\n┌──────────────┐         ┌───────┬───────┬───────┬───────┬───────┐\n│ arr: 0x7A01  │ ──────> │ 10    │ 20    │ 30    │ 40    │ 50    │\n└──────────────┘         └───────┴───────┴───────┴───────┴───────┘\n                          Index 0 Index 1 Index 2 Index 3 Index 4\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Key Properties of Java Arrays\n* **Index-Based**: 0 to length - 1. Accessing outside throws `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`.\n* **Fixed Size**: Length cannot be altered after creation. `arr.length` is an immutable field.\n* **Default Values**: Numeric types initialize to 0, booleans to false, reference objects to null.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates 1D Array declaration, allocation, traversal, and statistics\npublic class OneDimensionalArrayDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Step 1: Declaration and Dynamic Heap Allocation\n        int[] scores = new int[5];\n\n        // Step 2: Population\n        scores[0] = 88;\n        scores[1] = 92;\n        scores[2] = 79;\n        scores[3] = 95;\n        scores[4] = 84;\n\n        // Step 3: Traversal and Aggregation\n        int sum = 0;\n        int max = scores[0];\n        int min = scores[0];\n\n        System.out.print(\"Array elements: \");\n        for (int i = 0; i < scores.length; i++) {\n            System.out.print(scores[i] + \" \");\n            sum += scores[i];\n            if (scores[i] > max) max = scores[i];\n            if (scores[i] < min) min = scores[i];\n        }\n        System.out.println();\n\n        double average = (double) sum / scores.length;\n        System.out.println(\"Total Sum   : \" + sum);\n        System.out.println(\"Average     : \" + average);\n        System.out.println(\"Maximum     : \" + max);\n        System.out.println(\"Minimum     : \" + min);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nArray lookup by index is O(1) constant time because the JVM calculates the physical memory address directly: Address = Base + (Index * ElementSize).",
  "87": "# 87. 2D Array in Java (Matrices & Row-Major Order)\n\n## 1. 2D Arrays Architecture\nA 2-Dimensional array in Java is implemented as an **array of arrays**. Each row is an independent 1D array reference stored in Heap memory.\n\n```\nmatrix (Reference)\n┌───────────┐\n│ 0xRow0    │ ───> [ 1,  2,  3 ]\n│ 0xRow1    │ ───> [ 4,  5,  6 ]\n│ 0xRow2    │ ───> [ 7,  8,  9 ]\n└───────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Common Matrix Operations\n* **Matrix Addition & Subtraction**: Performed on matching dimension matrices.\n* **Matrix Transposition**: Swapping rows with columns (A[i][j] <-> A[j][i]).\n* **Diagonal Traversal**: Primary diagonal where i == j.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates 2D Matrix traversal, Addition, and Transposition\npublic class TwoDimensionalMatrixDemo {\n    public static void printMatrix(int[][] matrix, String title) {\n        System.out.println(\"--- \" + title + \" ---\");\n        for (int i = 0; i < matrix.length; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j < matrix[i].length; j++) {\n                System.out.printf(\"%4d\", matrix[i][j]);\n            }\n            System.out.println();\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static int[][] transpose(int[][] matrix) {\n        int rows = matrix.length;\n        int cols = matrix[0].length;\n        int[][] result = new int[cols][rows];\n        for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j < cols; j++) {\n                result[j][i] = matrix[i][j];\n            }\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[][] matA = {\n            {1, 2, 3},\n            {4, 5, 6},\n            {7, 8, 9}\n        };\n\n        printMatrix(matA, \"Original Matrix A\");\n        int[][] transposed = transpose(matA);\n        printMatrix(transposed, \"Transposed Matrix (Rows <-> Columns)\");\n\n        // Sum of primary diagonal\n        int diagSum = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < matA.length; i++) {\n            diagSum += matA[i][i];\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"Primary Diagonal Sum (1 + 5 + 9) = \" + diagSum);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nSince 2D arrays in Java are arrays of array references, individual rows do not necessarily have to be stored contiguously with each other in physical RAM.",
  "88": "# 88. 3D Array in Java (Tensors & Multidimensional Coordinates)\n\n## 1. 3D Array Architecture\nA 3D array in Java is an **array of 2D arrays**, representing three spatial dimensions: **Layers (Depth), Rows, and Columns**: `arr[layer][row][col]`.\n\n```\nLayer 0:           Layer 1:\n[ 1, 2 ]           [ 5, 6 ]\n[ 3, 4 ]           [ 7, 8 ]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Real-World Applications\n* **Spatial Game Engines**: 3D Voxel worlds (like Minecraft).\n* **Color Image Processing**: Height * Width * RGB Color Channels (3 planes: Red, Green, Blue).\n* **Time-Series Sensor Grids**: Sensors * Metrics * Time intervals.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates 3D Array modeling a multi-floor sensor temperature network\npublic class ThreeDimensionalArrayDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Dimensions: [Building Floors: 2][Rooms per floor: 2][Sensors per room: 3]\n        int[][][] sensorReadings = {\n            {\n                {22, 23, 22}, // Floor 0, Room 0\n                {24, 25, 24}  // Floor 0, Room 1\n            },\n            {\n                {20, 21, 20}, // Floor 1, Room 0\n                {19, 18, 19}  // Floor 1, Room 1\n            }\n        };\n\n        System.out.println(\"=== 3D Smart Building Sensor Grid ===\");\n        for (int floor = 0; floor < sensorReadings.length; floor++) {\n            System.out.println(\"Floor #\" + floor + \":\");\n            for (int room = 0; room < sensorReadings[floor].length; room++) {\n                System.out.print(\"  Room \" + room + \" Sensors: \");\n                for (int sensor = 0; sensor < sensorReadings[floor][room].length; sensor++) {\n                    System.out.print(sensorReadings[floor][room][sensor] + \"°C \");\n                }\n                System.out.println();\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nIn a 3D array `arr[D][R][C]`, `arr.length` gives depth (layers), `arr[i].length` gives row count of that layer, and `arr[i][j].length` gives column count.",
  "89": "# 89. Matrix, Jagged & Anonymous Arrays\n\n## 1. Jagged (Ragged) Arrays\nA **Jagged Array** is a multidimensional array where each row has a different number of columns. This eliminates wasted memory when data is non-uniform.\n\n```\njagged (Reference)\n┌───────────┐\n│ row 0     │ ───> [ 1, 2 ]             (Length: 2)\n│ row 1     │ ───> [ 3, 4, 5, 6 ]       (Length: 4)\n│ row 2     │ ───> [ 7 ]                (Length: 1)\n└───────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Anonymous Arrays\nAn **Anonymous Array** is created on the fly without assigning its reference to a variable name. It is passed directly as a method argument: `new int[]{1, 2, 3}`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates Jagged Array allocation and Anonymous Array method dispatch\npublic class JaggedAndAnonymousArrayDemo {\n    public static void printReport(int[] numbers) {\n        System.out.print(\"Processing Anonymous Array payload: \");\n        for (int n : numbers) System.out.print(n + \" \");\n        System.out.println();\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"=== 1. Jagged Array Demonstration ===\");\n        int[][] jagged = new int[3][];\n        jagged[0] = new int[]{10, 20};               // 2 elements\n        jagged[1] = new int[]{30, 40, 50, 60};       // 4 elements\n        jagged[2] = new int[]{70, 80, 90};           // 3 elements\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < jagged.length; i++) {\n            System.out.print(\"Row \" + i + \" (Cols: \" + jagged[i].length + \"): \");\n            for (int j = 0; j < jagged[i].length; j++) {\n                System.out.print(jagged[i][j] + \" \");\n            }\n            System.out.println();\n        }\n\n        System.out.println(\"\\n=== 2. Anonymous Array Demonstration ===\");\n        printReport(new int[]{100, 200, 300, 400});\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nJagged arrays optimize memory efficiency for triangular matrix problems, Pascal's triangle, or uneven grouping (e.g. employee shifts per department).",
  "90": "# 90. Array Programs - Part 1: Searching Algorithms\n\n## 1. Searching Problem Statement\nSearching involves locating the index of a specified target key within an array.\n\n```\nLinear Search:   Checks index 0, 1, 2, 3... sequentially.  Time: O(N)\nBinary Search:   Halves search interval each step.         Time: O(log N) - Requires Sorted Array\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Step-by-Step Binary Search Trace\nGiven array `[12, 24, 36, 48, 60, 72, 84]` and target `60`:\n1. `low = 0, high = 6`, `mid = 3` (`arr[3] = 48`). 48 < 60 → shift `low = mid + 1 = 4`.\n2. `low = 4, high = 6`, `mid = 5` (`arr[5] = 72`). 72 > 60 → shift `high = mid - 1 = 4`.\n3. `low = 4, high = 4`, `mid = 4` (`arr[4] = 60`). Match found at index 4!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates Linear Search and Binary Search with step counter\npublic class SearchAlgorithmsDemo {\n    public static int linearSearch(int[] arr, int target) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\n            if (arr[i] == target) return i;\n        }\n        return -1;\n    }\n\n    public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {\n        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;\n        int steps = 0;\n        while (low <= high) {\n            steps++;\n            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2; // Prevents 32-bit integer overflow!\n            if (arr[mid] == target) {\n                System.out.println(\"[Binary Search] Found in \" + steps + \" comparisons!\");\n                return mid;\n            } else if (arr[mid] < target) {\n                low = mid + 1;\n            } else {\n                high = mid - 1;\n            }\n        }\n        return -1;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] sortedData = {15, 28, 33, 47, 59, 66, 74, 88, 92, 105};\n\n        System.out.println(\"Linear Search 74 : Index \" + linearSearch(sortedData, 74));\n        System.out.println(\"Binary Search 74 : Index \" + binarySearch(sortedData, 74));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nAlways calculate `mid` as `low + (high - low) / 2` rather than `(low + high) / 2` to protect against integer arithmetic overflow when arrays contain millions of elements.",
  "91": "# 91. Array Programs - Part 2: Sorting Algorithms\n\n## 1. Classical Sorting Algorithms\nSorting arranges elements in ascending or descending order.\n\n```\n+----------------+----------------+----------------+-------------------+\n| Algorithm      | Best Time      | Worst Time     | Space Complexity  |\n+----------------+----------------+----------------+-------------------+\n| Bubble Sort    | O(N) (Optimized| O(N^2)         | O(1) In-place     |\n| Selection Sort | O(N^2)         | O(N^2)         | O(1) In-place     |\n| Insertion Sort | O(N)           | O(N^2)         | O(1) In-place     |\n+----------------+----------------+----------------+-------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates Bubble Sort (with early exit), Selection Sort, and Insertion Sort\nimport java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class SortAlgorithmsDemo {\n    public static void bubbleSort(int[] arr) {\n        int n = arr.length;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n            boolean swapped = false;\n            for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {\n                if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {\n                    int temp = arr[j];\n                    arr[j] = arr[j + 1];\n                    arr[j + 1] = temp;\n                    swapped = true;\n                }\n            }\n            if (!swapped) break; // Array is already sorted, exit early\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void selectionSort(int[] arr) {\n        int n = arr.length;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n            int minIdx = i;\n            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n                if (arr[j] < arr[minIdx]) minIdx = j;\n            }\n            int temp = arr[minIdx];\n            arr[minIdx] = arr[i];\n            arr[i] = temp;\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] data1 = {64, 25, 12, 22, 11};\n        int[] data2 = {64, 25, 12, 22, 11};\n\n        bubbleSort(data1);\n        System.out.println(\"Bubble Sort Result   : \" + Arrays.toString(data1));\n\n        selectionSort(data2);\n        System.out.println(\"Selection Sort Result: \" + Arrays.toString(data2));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nInsertion Sort is the algorithm of choice for small or nearly sorted datasets, forming the internal foundation for Java's hybrid **TimSort** (`Arrays.sort()`).",
  "92": "# 92. Array Programs - Part 3: Advanced Array Problems\n\n## 1. Critical Interview Patterns\n* **Two-Pointer Reversal**: Swapping elements from both ends moving inward in O(N) time and O(1) space.\n* **Kadane's Algorithm**: Maximum contiguous subarray sum in optimal O(N) single-pass time.\n* **Array Rotation**: Shifting elements by K positions using modular reversal.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates Two-Pointer In-Place Reversal and Kadane's Algorithm\nimport java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class AdvancedArrayAlgorithmsDemo {\n    public static void reverse(int[] arr) {\n        int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;\n        while (left < right) {\n            int temp = arr[left];\n            arr[left] = arr[right];\n            arr[right] = temp;\n            left++;\n            right--;\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static int maxSubArrayKadane(int[] nums) {\n        int maxSoFar = nums[0];\n        int currentMax = nums[0];\n        for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            currentMax = Math.max(nums[i], currentMax + nums[i]);\n            maxSoFar = Math.max(maxSoFar, currentMax);\n        }\n        return maxSoFar;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n        reverse(numbers);\n        System.out.println(\"Reversed Array: \" + Arrays.toString(numbers));\n\n        int[] kadaneInput = {-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4};\n        int maxSum = maxSubArrayKadane(kadaneInput);\n        System.out.println(\"Kadane's Maximum Subarray Sum: \" + maxSum + \" (Subarray: [4, -1, 2, 1])\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nKadane's algorithm reduces an intuitive O(N^2) brute-force problem down to an optimal single-pass O(N) by resetting the current running sum whenever it dips below zero.",
  "93": "# 93. Java Collections Framework (JCF) Architecture\n\n## 1. The Need for Collections\nStandard Java arrays have fixed sizes, lack built-in search/sort helpers, and do not handle dynamic heterogeneous data effortlessly. The **Java Collections Framework (JCF)** provides a unified architecture with dynamic resizing, high performance, and standard interfaces.\n\n```\n                           Iterable<E>\n                                │\n                          Collection<E>\n        ┌───────────────────────┼───────────────────────┐\n        ▼                       ▼                       ▼\n     List<E>                 Queue<E>                 Set<E>\n  (ArrayList,              (PriorityQueue,          (HashSet,\n   LinkedList,              ArrayDeque)              TreeSet)\n   Vector)\n                                              Map<K, V> (Separate Hierarchy)\n                                              (HashMap, TreeMap, LinkedHashMap)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates Collection hierarchy polymorphism and basic container behaviors\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class CollectionHierarchyDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Polymorphic reference: Program to interface, not implementation\n        Collection<String> items = new ArrayList<>();\n        items.add(\"Spring Boot\");\n        items.add(\"Kafka\");\n        items.add(\"Docker\");\n\n        System.out.println(\"Total items count : \" + items.size());\n        System.out.println(\"Contains 'Docker' : \" + items.contains(\"Docker\"));\n\n        Object[] array = items.toArray();\n        System.out.println(\"Converted to array: \" + Arrays.toString(array));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\n`Map` is not a child of `Collection` because a Map holds key-value pairs (`Entry<K, V>`), whereas a Collection holds isolated individual elements (`E`).",
  "94": "# 94. Collection Interface Core Methods\n\n## 1. Universal Methods on All Collections\nThe root `Collection<E>` interface defines common operations guaranteed across Lists, Sets, and Queues:\n* `add(E e)`, `addAll(Collection c)`: Insert elements.\n* `remove(Object o)`, `removeAll(Collection c)`: Delete elements.\n* `contains(Object o)`, `containsAll(Collection c)`: Membership query.\n* `retainAll(Collection c)`: Set intersection.\n* `size()`, `isEmpty()`, `clear()`: Capacity management.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates bulk operations on Collection interface\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Collection;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class CollectionMethodsDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Collection<String> groupA = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"Java\", \"Python\", \"Go\"));\n        Collection<String> groupB = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"Python\", \"Go\", \"Rust\"));\n\n        // Intersection via retainAll\n        groupA.retainAll(groupB);\n        System.out.println(\"Common Languages (Intersection): \" + groupA); // [Python, Go]\n\n        // Union via addAll\n        Collection<String> union = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"Java\", \"Python\"));\n        union.addAll(groupB);\n        System.out.println(\"Combined Elements (Union)       : \" + union);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nUsing `retainAll` produces the set mathematical intersection without needing manual nested loops.",
  "95": "# 95. List Interface & ArrayList Class\n\n## 1. ArrayList Deep Dive\n`ArrayList` is a dynamically resizable array. When internal capacity is exceeded, it allocates a new larger array (typically 1.5 * current size) and copies elements over.\n\n```\nIndex:    0    1    2    3    4    (Capacity: 10)\nItems:  [ 10 | 20 | 30 |    |    ]\nLookup: O(1) by index\nAdd:    Amortized O(1) at tail, O(N) when inserting in middle\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates ArrayList dynamic operations, capacity growth, and indexing\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class ArrayListDeepDiveDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<String> cities = new ArrayList<>(3); // Initial capacity 3\n\n        cities.add(\"San Francisco\");\n        cities.add(\"New York\");\n        cities.add(\"Tokyo\");\n        cities.add(\"London\"); // Dynamically resizes internally\n\n        System.out.println(\"Cities List: \" + cities);\n        System.out.println(\"City at index 2: \" + cities.get(2)); // O(1) random access\n\n        cities.add(1, \"Paris\");\n        System.out.println(\"After inserting Paris at index 1: \" + cities);\n\n        cities.remove(\"New York\");\n        System.out.println(\"After removing New York: \" + cities);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nAlways provide an initial capacity hint `new ArrayList<>(expectedSize)` if the volume is known ahead of time to prevent multiple expensive internal array allocations.",
  "96": "# 96. ArrayList Part 2: Custom Objects & Sorting\n\n## 1. Managing Domain Entities in Lists\nEnterprise systems store domain entity objects (e.g. `Product`, `Employee`) in lists.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates managing custom class objects inside an ArrayList\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Collections;\nimport java.util.Comparator;\nimport java.util.List;\n\nclass Student {\n    private int id;\n    private String name;\n    private double gpa;\n\n    public Student(int id, String name, double gpa) {\n        this.id = id;\n        this.name = name;\n        this.gpa = gpa;\n    }\n\n    public int getId() { return id; }\n    public String getName() { return name; }\n    public double getGpa() { return gpa; }\n\n    @Override\n    public String toString() {\n        return \"[\" + id + \"] \" + name + \" (GPA: \" + gpa + \")\";\n    }\n}\n\npublic class ArrayListCustomObjectsDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<Student> roster = new ArrayList<>();\n        roster.add(new Student(101, \"Alice\", 3.9));\n        roster.add(new Student(102, \"Bob\", 3.4));\n        roster.add(new Student(103, \"Charlie\", 3.8));\n\n        // Sort students by GPA descending using Comparator\n        roster.sort(Comparator.comparingDouble(Student::getGpa).reversed());\n\n        System.out.println(\"=== Students Ranked by GPA ===\");\n        for (Student s : roster) {\n            System.out.println(\"  \" + s);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nUse `list.sort(Comparator.comparing(...))` for clean, readable, in-place sorting without mutating your data structures externally.",
  "97": "# 97. LinkedList Class & Doubly-Linked Architecture\n\n## 1. LinkedList Internals\nUnlike `ArrayList`, Java's `LinkedList` is implemented as a **Doubly-Linked List**. Each element is wrapped inside a `Node` containing: `E item`, `Node next`, and `Node prev`.\n\n```\n        Node 1                    Node 2                    Node 3\n┌──────┬────┬──────┐      ┌──────┬────┬──────┐      ┌──────┬────┬──────┐\n│ null │ 10 │ 0x20 │ <──> │ 0x10 │ 20 │ 0x30 │ <──> │ 0x20 │ 30 │ null │\n└──────┴────┴──────┘      └──────┴────┴──────┘      └──────┴────┴──────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates LinkedList as both a List and a Deque (Double-Ended Queue)\nimport java.util.LinkedList;\n\npublic class LinkedListDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        LinkedList<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();\n\n        // Deque operations: O(1) insertion at head and tail\n        queue.addFirst(\"Priority Emergency Request\");\n        queue.addLast(\"Standard Ticket #1\");\n        queue.addLast(\"Standard Ticket #2\");\n\n        System.out.println(\"LinkedList State: \" + queue);\n        System.out.println(\"Peek First Item : \" + queue.peekFirst());\n\n        String processed = queue.removeFirst();\n        System.out.println(\"Processed Item  : \" + processed);\n        System.out.println(\"Remaining Queue : \" + queue);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nPrefer `ArrayList` for frequent random read access (`get(i)` is O(1)). Prefer `LinkedList` or `ArrayDeque` when constantly adding or removing from the beginning of the list (O(1) vs O(N)).",
  "98": "# 98. Vector Legacy Class (Thread-Safe List)\n\n## 1. Vector Overview\n`Vector` is a legacy synchronized dynamic array introduced in Java 1.0. Every method in `Vector` is synchronized, meaning only one thread can access it at a time.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates Vector thread-safe operations and capacity increment\nimport java.util.Enumeration;\nimport java.util.Vector;\n\npublic class VectorLegacyDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Vector<String> vector = new Vector<>(4, 2);\n\n        vector.add(\"Data-1\");\n        vector.add(\"Data-2\");\n        vector.add(\"Data-3\");\n        vector.add(\"Data-4\");\n        vector.add(\"Data-5\"); // Expands capacity by 2 (4 + 2 = 6)\n\n        System.out.println(\"Vector Size    : \" + vector.size());\n        System.out.println(\"Vector Capacity: \" + vector.capacity());\n\n        System.out.print(\"Enumeration: \");\n        Enumeration<String> en = vector.elements();\n        while (en.hasMoreElements()) {\n            System.out.print(en.nextElement() + \" \");\n        }\n        System.out.println();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nIn modern concurrent applications, prefer `CopyOnWriteArrayList` or `Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<>())` over legacy `Vector`.",
  "99": "# 99. Stack Legacy Class (LIFO Architecture)\n\n## 1. Stack Principles\n`Stack` implements **Last-In, First-Out (LIFO)** data storage.\n* `push(E item)`: Pushes onto top of stack.\n* `pop()`: Removes and returns top item.\n* `peek()`: Inspects top item without removing it.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates Stack using Balanced Parentheses Expression Validation\nimport java.util.Stack;\n\npublic class StackBalancedParenthesesDemo {\n    public static boolean isBalanced(String expr) {\n        Stack<Character> stack = new Stack<>();\n        for (char ch : expr.toCharArray()) {\n            if (ch == '(' || ch == '{' || ch == '[') {\n                stack.push(ch);\n            } else if (ch == ')' || ch == '}' || ch == ']') {\n                if (stack.isEmpty()) return false;\n                char top = stack.pop();\n                if ((ch == ')' && top != '(') ||\n                    (ch == '}' && top != '{') ||\n                    (ch == ']' && top != '[')) {\n                    return false;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return stack.isEmpty();\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String test1 = \"{[()]}\";\n        String test2 = \"{[(])}\";\n\n        System.out.println(\"Expression \" + test1 + \" Balanced? \" + isBalanced(test1));\n        System.out.println(\"Expression \" + test2 + \" Balanced? \" + isBalanced(test2));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nJava official documentation recommends using `ArrayDeque` as a stack instead of legacy `Stack`: `Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();`.",
  "100": "# 100. Cursors in Java Collections (Iterator, ListIterator & Enumeration)\n\n## 1. The Three Cursors\n```\n+---------------+---------------------+--------------------+--------------------+\n| Cursor        | Applicable On       | Direction          | Modification Ops   |\n+---------------+---------------------+--------------------+--------------------+\n| Enumeration   | Legacy (Vector/Map) | Forward Only       | Read-only          |\n| Iterator      | All Collections     | Forward Only       | Read + remove()    |\n| ListIterator  | List implementations| Bidirectional      | Read, remove, add, |\n|               | (ArrayList, etc.)   | (previous/next)    | set()              |\n+---------------+---------------------+--------------------+--------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Fail-Fast vs ConcurrentModificationException\nIf a collection is structurally modified directly while iterating without using the cursor's own `iterator.remove()` method, the JVM throws `ConcurrentModificationException`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates Iterator safe removal and ListIterator bidirectional traversal\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Iterator;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.ListIterator;\n\npublic class CollectionCursorsDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<String> logs = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"INFO: 200\", \"DEBUG: Trace\", \"ERROR: 500\", \"DEBUG: Mem\"));\n\n        Iterator<String> it = logs.iterator();\n        while (it.hasNext()) {\n            String entry = it.next();\n            if (entry.startsWith(\"DEBUG\")) {\n                it.remove(); // Safe removal without ConcurrentModificationException!\n            }\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"After Iterator cleanup: \" + logs);\n\n        ListIterator<String> listIt = logs.listIterator(logs.size());\n        System.out.print(\"Reverse Traversal: \");\n        while (listIt.hasPrevious()) {\n            System.out.print(\"[\" + listIt.previous() + \"] \");\n        }\n        System.out.println();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nNever modify a collection using `list.remove(item)` inside a `for-each` loop; always use `Iterator.remove()` or modern `list.removeIf(predicate)`.",
  "101": "# 101. Set Interface & HashSet Class\n\n## 1. Set Contract & Hashing Internals\nA `Set` cannot contain duplicate elements. `HashSet` stores elements backed by an internal `HashMap<E, Object>`.\n* **Hash Calculation**: `element.hashCode()` produces a 32-bit integer bucket index.\n* **Equality Verification**: If bucket already has elements (hash collision), `equals()` determines if item is truly identical.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates HashSet uniqueness and overriding hashCode() and equals()\nimport java.util.HashSet;\nimport java.util.Objects;\nimport java.util.Set;\n\nclass UserKey {\n    private String email;\n\n    public UserKey(String email) { this.email = email; }\n\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        if (this == o) return true;\n        if (!(o instanceof UserKey)) return false;\n        UserKey userKey = (UserKey) o;\n        return Objects.equals(email, userKey.email);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int hashCode() {\n        return Objects.hash(email);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public String toString() { return email; }\n}\n\npublic class HashSetInternalsDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set<UserKey> activeUsers = new HashSet<>();\n\n        activeUsers.add(new UserKey(\"admin@corp.io\"));\n        activeUsers.add(new UserKey(\"support@corp.io\"));\n        activeUsers.add(new UserKey(\"admin@corp.io\")); // Duplicate email rejected!\n\n        System.out.println(\"Total Unique Users (Expected 2): \" + activeUsers.size());\n        System.out.println(\"Active Users Set: \" + activeUsers);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nWhenever you override `equals()`, you **MUST** override `hashCode()`. Violating this contract breaks `HashSet` and `HashMap` lookups.",
  "102": "# 102. LinkedHashSet, SortedSet & NavigableSet\n\n## 1. Choosing the Right Set\n* **`HashSet`**: Unordered, unsorted, fastest (O(1) operations).\n* **`LinkedHashSet`**: Maintains **Insertion Order** via a doubly-linked bucket list (O(1) operations).\n* **`TreeSet`**: Implements `SortedSet` and `NavigableSet`, maintaining elements in sorted ascending order (O(log N) operations).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates Insertion-order preservation and NavigableSet range queries\nimport java.util.LinkedHashSet;\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class SetImplementationsDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set<String> flow = new LinkedHashSet<>();\n        flow.add(\"Step 1: Auth\");\n        flow.add(\"Step 2: Payment\");\n        flow.add(\"Step 3: Receipt\");\n        System.out.println(\"LinkedHashSet maintains order: \" + flow);\n\n        TreeSet<Integer> scores = new TreeSet<>(Set.of(45, 62, 78, 85, 91, 99));\n        System.out.println(\"Scores sorted: \" + scores);\n        System.out.println(\"First score  : \" + scores.first());\n        System.out.println(\"Last score   : \" + scores.last());\n        System.out.println(\"Ceiling(70)  : \" + scores.ceiling(70)); // 78 (lowest element >= 70)\n        System.out.println(\"Floor(70)    : \" + scores.floor(70));   // 62 (highest element <= 70)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nUse `LinkedHashSet` when you require uniqueness without losing the exact chronological sequence of insertion.",
  "103": "# 103. TreeSet Class & Red-Black Tree Architecture\n\n## 1. Red-Black Tree Internals\n`TreeSet` is implemented using a self-balancing binary search tree (**Red-Black Tree**). It guarantees O(log N) time for `add()`, `remove()`, and `contains()`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates TreeSet sorted storage and subSet range views\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetRedBlackDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<Integer> ports = new TreeSet<>();\n        ports.add(8080);\n        ports.add(22);\n        ports.add(443);\n        ports.add(3306);\n        ports.add(80);\n\n        System.out.println(\"Automatically Sorted Ports: \" + ports);\n        System.out.println(\"Standard Web Ports (80 to 8080): \" + ports.subSet(80, 8080));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\n`TreeSet` does not allow null elements because it must compare items using `compareTo()`, which would throw `NullPointerException`.",
  "104": "# 104. Comparable vs Comparator in Java\n\n## 1. Comparison Contract\n```\n+--------------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------+\n| Aspect             | Comparable<T>                  | Comparator<T>                  |\n+--------------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------+\n| Package            | java.lang                      | java.util                      |\n| Method             | int compareTo(T o)             | int compare(T o1, T o2)        |\n| Purpose            | Natural default ordering       | Custom external sort strategies|\n| Code Location      | Inside domain class            | Separate class or lambda       |\n| Multiple Sorts?    | Only 1 ordering per class      | Unlimited sort criteria        |\n+--------------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates Comparable (Natural Order by ID) and Comparator (Custom Order by Price)\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Collections;\nimport java.util.Comparator;\nimport java.util.List;\n\nclass Product implements Comparable<Product> {\n    private int id;\n    private String title;\n    private double price;\n\n    public Product(int id, String title, double price) {\n        this.id = id;\n        this.title = title;\n        this.price = price;\n    }\n\n    public double getPrice() { return price; }\n\n    @Override\n    public int compareTo(Product other) {\n        return Integer.compare(this.id, other.id); // Natural order by ID\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public String toString() {\n        return \"[\" + id + \"] \" + title + \" ($\" + price + \")\";\n    }\n}\n\npublic class ComparableComparatorDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<Product> catalog = new ArrayList<>();\n        catalog.add(new Product(301, \"Monitor 4K\", 399.99));\n        catalog.add(new Product(102, \"USB-C Hub\", 29.99));\n        catalog.add(new Product(205, \"Wireless Mouse\", 49.99));\n\n        Collections.sort(catalog);\n        System.out.println(\"Sorted by Natural ID   : \" + catalog);\n\n        catalog.sort(Comparator.comparingDouble(Product::getPrice));\n        System.out.println(\"Sorted by Custom Price : \" + catalog);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nAlways use `Double.compare()` or `Integer.compare()` in comparison methods rather than subtraction (`a - b`) to prevent numeric overflow bugs with extreme integers.",
  "105": "# 105. Queue Interface & PriorityQueue Class\n\n## 1. Queue Architecture\nA `Queue` operates on **FIFO (First-In, First-Out)** principles.\n* `offer(e)`: Inserts item (safe, returns boolean).\n* `poll()`: Retrieves and removes head (returns null if empty).\n* `peek()`: Inspects head without removal.\n\n`PriorityQueue` orders elements by priority using a **Binary Min-Heap**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates PriorityQueue processing tasks by priority severity\nimport java.util.PriorityQueue;\n\nclass ServerTask implements Comparable<ServerTask> {\n    private String taskName;\n    private int severity; // 1 = Critical, 5 = Low\n\n    public ServerTask(String name, int severity) {\n        this.taskName = name;\n        this.severity = severity;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int compareTo(ServerTask o) {\n        return Integer.compare(this.severity, o.severity); // Lower integer = Higher priority\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public String toString() {\n        return \"Priority \" + severity + \": \" + taskName;\n    }\n}\n\npublic class PriorityQueueDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        PriorityQueue<ServerTask> queue = new PriorityQueue<>();\n\n        queue.offer(new ServerTask(\"Cache warming\", 4));\n        queue.offer(new ServerTask(\"Database Disk Full\", 1));\n        queue.offer(new ServerTask(\"Rotate logs\", 3));\n\n        System.out.println(\"=== Min-Heap Task Dispatch Order ===\");\n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            System.out.println(\"Processing: \" + queue.poll());\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\n`PriorityQueue` `peek()` is O(1) and `offer()` / `poll()` are O(log N). It does not guarantee internal array ordering, only that `poll()` yields the minimum element.",
  "106": "# 106. Deque Interface & ArrayDeque Class\n\n## 1. Double-Ended Queue (Deque)\nA **Deque** supports element insertion and removal at both ends. It is faster than `Stack` when used as a LIFO container and faster than `LinkedList` when used as a FIFO queue.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates ArrayDeque as a browser history navigation stack\nimport java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\n\npublic class ArrayDequeBrowserHistoryDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Deque<String> history = new ArrayDeque<>();\n\n        history.push(\"google.com\");\n        history.push(\"github.com\");\n        history.push(\"stackoverflow.com\");\n\n        System.out.println(\"Current Page: \" + history.peek());\n\n        System.out.println(\"Clicked Back: Left \" + history.pop());\n        System.out.println(\"Now viewing : \" + history.peek());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\n`ArrayDeque` has zero node object allocation overhead compared to `LinkedList`, making it significantly friendlier on JVM Garbage Collection.",
  "107": "# 107. Entry Interface & HashMap Class Internals\n\n## 1. HashMap Architecture Deep Dive\nA `HashMap` stores data as `Map.Entry<K, V>` in an array of **Buckets**.\n1. **Hash Calculation**: `key.hashCode()` spreads keys across 16 default buckets.\n2. **Collision Resolution**: Handled via Linked List. If bucket chain length >= 8, Java 8+ converts bucket to a **Red-Black Tree** (**Treeify**), boosting worst-case lookup from O(N) to O(log N).\n3. **Load Factor & Rehashing**: Default load factor is 0.75. When 75% of bucket capacity is reached, table doubles in size.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates HashMap key-value lookups, Entry set iteration, and getOrDefault\nimport java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\npublic class HashMapDeepDiveDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Map<String, Integer> stockInventory = new HashMap<>();\n\n        stockInventory.put(\"SKU-LAPTOP\", 120);\n        stockInventory.put(\"SKU-MOUSE\", 450);\n        stockInventory.put(\"SKU-KEYBOARD\", 230);\n\n        int monitorStock = stockInventory.getOrDefault(\"SKU-MONITOR\", 0);\n        System.out.println(\"Monitor stock count: \" + monitorStock);\n\n        System.out.println(\"=== Inventory Ledger ===\");\n        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : stockInventory.entrySet()) {\n            System.out.println(\"SKU: \" + entry.getKey() + \" -> Units: \" + entry.getValue());\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\n`HashMap` permits one null key (stored at bucket 0) and multiple null values. Average lookup, insertion, and deletion are O(1).",
  "108": "# 108. LinkedHashMap, IdentityHashMap & WeakHashMap\n\n## 1. Specialized Map Implementations\n* **`LinkedHashMap`**: Maintains insertion order or **Access Order** (ideal for building **LRU Caches**).\n* **`IdentityHashMap`**: Compares keys via reference equality (`k1 == k2`) rather than `equals()`.\n* **`WeakHashMap`**: Stores keys as `WeakReference`. If a key is no longer referenced elsewhere, the JVM Garbage Collector reclaims it automatically.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates building a 3-item Least Recently Used (LRU) Cache via LinkedHashMap\nimport java.util.LinkedHashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\nclass LruCache<K, V> extends LinkedHashMap<K, V> {\n    private final int maxEntries;\n\n    public LruCache(int capacity) {\n        super(capacity, 0.75f, true); // true = access-order instead of insertion-order\n        this.maxEntries = capacity;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, V> eldest) {\n        return size() > maxEntries; // Automatically evicts oldest item when size exceeds limit\n    }\n}\n\npublic class SpecializedMapsDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        LruCache<String, String> cache = new LruCache<>(3);\n\n        cache.put(\"user1\", \"Alice\");\n        cache.put(\"user2\", \"Bob\");\n        cache.put(\"user3\", \"Charlie\");\n\n        cache.get(\"user1\"); // Access user1 to make it most recently used\n        cache.put(\"user4\", \"Dave\"); // 4th item forces eviction of eldest (user2)\n\n        System.out.println(\"LRU Cache after access & eviction: \" + cache);\n        System.out.println(\"Did user2 get evicted? \" + !cache.containsKey(\"user2\"));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nOverriding `removeEldestEntry()` in `LinkedHashMap` allows creating a production-ready LRU cache in under 10 lines of code.",
  "109": "# 109. SortedMap & NavigableMap (TreeMap Deep Dive)\n\n## 1. Navigable Key-Value Maps\n`TreeMap` implements `SortedMap` and `NavigableMap`. Keys are maintained in sorted order via their natural ordering or a `Comparator`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates TreeMap range lookups and subMap slicing\nimport java.util.TreeMap;\n\npublic class TreeMapNavigableDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeMap<Integer, String> employeeTiers = new TreeMap<>();\n\n        employeeTiers.put(30000, \"Junior Associate\");\n        employeeTiers.put(60000, \"Mid-level Engineer\");\n        employeeTiers.put(95000, \"Senior Staff Engineer\");\n        employeeTiers.put(140000, \"Principal Architect\");\n\n        System.out.println(\"Salary Bracket Map: \" + employeeTiers);\n        System.out.println(\"Role for $70,000 (Floor): \" + employeeTiers.floorEntry(70000).getValue());\n        System.out.println(\"Slicing $50k to $100k   : \" + employeeTiers.subMap(50000, 100000));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\n`TreeMap` performance is O(log N) for all basic operations (`get`, `put`, `remove`). Keys cannot be null.",
  "110": "# 110. Hashtable Class (Legacy Synchronized Map)\n\n## 1. Hashtable Overview\n`Hashtable` is a Java 1.0 legacy key-value store.\n* Every method is synchronized.\n* **Does NOT allow null keys or null values** (throws `NullPointerException`).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates Hashtable synchronized properties\nimport java.util.Hashtable;\n\npublic class HashtableLegacyDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Hashtable<String, String> lookup = new Hashtable<>();\n\n        lookup.put(\"DB_HOST\", \"127.0.0.1\");\n        lookup.put(\"DB_PORT\", \"5432\");\n\n        System.out.println(\"Hashtable Config: \" + lookup);\n        System.out.println(\"Contains 'DB_PORT': \" + lookup.containsKey(\"DB_PORT\"));\n\n        try {\n            lookup.put(null, \"invalid\"); // Fails!\n        } catch (NullPointerException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"Caught expected NullPointerException: Hashtable prohibits null keys!\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nIn modern multi-threaded applications, never use `Hashtable`. Use `ConcurrentHashMap` for high concurrency without full-table locks.",
  "111": "# 111. Dictionary, Properties & Concurrent Classes\n\n## 1. Configuration Management with Properties\nThe `Properties` class extends `Hashtable<Object, Object>` and represents a persistent set of string configuration properties. It is the industry standard for reading `.properties` files.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates Properties configuration loading and ConcurrentHashMap thread safety\nimport java.io.ByteArrayInputStream;\nimport java.io.IOException;\nimport java.util.Properties;\nimport java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;\n\npublic class PropertiesAndConcurrentDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        String mockConfigFile = \"server.port=8080\\nserver.environment=production\\ncache.enabled=true\";\n        Properties props = new Properties();\n        props.load(new ByteArrayInputStream(mockConfigFile.getBytes()));\n\n        System.out.println(\"Port from Properties: \" + props.getProperty(\"server.port\"));\n        System.out.println(\"Environment         : \" + props.getProperty(\"server.environment\"));\n\n        ConcurrentHashMap<String, Integer> atomicCounters = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n        atomicCounters.put(\"page_views\", 100);\n        atomicCounters.compute(\"page_views\", (k, v) -> v + 1);\n        System.out.println(\"Concurrent atomic counter: \" + atomicCounters.get(\"page_views\"));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\n`ConcurrentHashMap` achieves concurrency through fine-grained bucket-level locks (synchronized on bucket head nodes) rather than locking the entire map.",
  "112": "# 112. Java Generics (Type Safety, Bounds & Wildcards)\n\n## 1. Why Generics?\nIntroduced in Java 5, Generics eliminate runtime `ClassCastException` by enforcing strong **Compile-Time Type Checking**.\n* **Type Parameter**: `Box<T>`.\n* **Bounded Type**: `<T extends Number>`.\n* **Wildcards**: Upper Bounded `<? extends Number>`, Lower Bounded `<? super Integer>`, Unbounded `<?>`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Java Class Demonstration\n```java\n// Demonstrates Generic Class, Bounded Type Parameter, and Wildcards\nimport java.util.List;\n\nclass DataContainer<T> {\n    private T item;\n    public void set(T item) { this.item = item; }\n    public T get() { return item; }\n}\n\npublic class GenericsMasteryDemo {\n    public static double sumOfNumbers(List<? extends Number> list) {\n        double sum = 0.0;\n        for (Number n : list) sum += n.doubleValue();\n        return sum;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        DataContainer<String> stringBox = new DataContainer<>();\n        stringBox.set(\"Enterprise Payload\");\n        String val = stringBox.get();\n        System.out.println(\"Retrieved from Generic Box: \" + val);\n\n        List<Integer> integers = List.of(10, 20, 30);\n        List<Double> doubles = List.of(1.5, 2.5, 3.5);\n\n        System.out.println(\"Sum of Integers (Wildcard): \" + sumOfNumbers(integers));\n        System.out.println(\"Sum of Doubles  (Wildcard): \" + sumOfNumbers(doubles));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nRemember the **PECS** rule: **Producer Extends, Consumer Super**. Use `? extends T` when reading data from a collection; use `? super T` when writing data into a collection."
};
